er_play":0.2127,"pass_rate":0.4688,"pass_rate_neutral":0.4426,"def_epa_per_play":-0.007718},{"week":9,"opponent":"PHI","home":false,"pf":19.0,"pa":11.0,"off_snaps":63,"epa_per_play":-0.06837,"pass_rate":0.5238,"pass_rate_neutral":0.5789,"def_epa_per_play":-0.2207},{"week":10,"opponent":"WAS","home":true,"pf":21.0,"pa":24.0,"off_snaps":63,"epa_per_play":0.07514,"pass_rate":0.5873,"pass_rate_neutral":0.6111,"def_epa_per_play":0.03882},{"week":11,"opponent":"JAX","home":true,"pf":23.0,"pa":18.0,"off_snaps":62,"epa_per_play":-0.1406,"pass_rate":0.6452,"pass_rate_neutral":0.5455,"def_epa_per_play":-0.09521}],"comps":{"offense":[{"season":2022,"team":"DEN","distance":0.4726,"shared":12,"play_caller":null},{"season":2025,"team":"LAC","distance":0.5789,"shared":12,"play_caller":"Greg Roman"},{"season":2022,"team":"PIT","distance":0.6456,"shared":12,"play_caller":null},{"season":2025,"team":"HOU","distance":0.6636,"shared":12,"play_caller":"Nick Caley"},{"season":2024,"team":"TEN","distance":0.6725,"shared":12,"play_caller":null}],"defense":[{"season":2022,"team":"CAR","distance":0.2875,"shared":4,"play_caller":null},{"season":2023,"team":"DAL","distance":0.3494,"shared":4,"play_caller":"Dan Quinn"},{"season":2025,"team":"TB","distance":0.3621,"shared":4,"play_caller":"Todd Bowles"},{"season":2025,"team":"NYJ","distance":0.3679,"shared":4,"play_caller":null},{"season":2025,"team":"PIT","distance":0.443,"shared":4,"play_caller":null}]},"overview":{"overview":"The Giants are 6-3-1 through week 11 of the 2026 season and have outscored opponents 226 to 214. The offense ranks 17th in EPA per play and the defense 13th in EPA allowed, so neither side has stood out much yet. What sets them apart is the 3rd most backs in the backfield and the 3rd most defenders in the box in the league.","offense":"The Giants offense has been about average by EPA per play so far. Their run and pass mix has stayed close to what game situations call for. Their biggest outliers in how they line up are the 3rd most backs in the backfield in the league. Jameis Winston has run the passing game and leaned heavily on Malik Nabers, who has drawn 29% of the targets.","defense":"The Giants defense has been a little above average by EPA allowed per play so far. Its strongest area has been takeaways per game, where it ranks 10th, and its weakest has been sack rate, where it ranks 24th. Arvell Reese has led the way with 4.5 sacks. Schematically, they have the 3rd most defenders in the box in the league.","special":"The Giants have been about average in how often they go for it on fourth down. Their kickers have made 74% of field goals, which is well below average. On the roster side, they put an unusually large share of their salary cap into linebackers, about 21% of the money on the books. Their defensive line was among the oldest in the league.","players":"The quarterback job has been split, with Jameis Winston and J.J. McCarthy both getting meaningful snaps. Malik Nabers has been the clear top target with 29% of the looks and 747 receiving yards. Cam Skattebo has handled most of the work in the backfield with 57% of the carries. On defense, Arvell Reese leads the team with 4.5 sacks and Jevon Holland has picked off 4 passes.","coaching":"Matt Nagy is calling the offense and Dennard Wilson is calling the defense. Their offense looks most like the 2022 Broncos and the 2025 Chargers under Greg Roman. Their defense looks most like the 2022 Panthers and the 2023 Cowboys under Dan Quinn.","trends":"The offense went from 17th in EPA per play in 2025 to 17th so far this season, about where it was. The defense moved from 25th in EPA allowed per play in 2025 to 13th so far this season, a big step forward. The biggest change in approach was number of defenders in the box, which went from 6.18 to 6.53.","notes":"The 2026 pages are built from data that nflverse updates every week, so they will keep changing until the season ends. Personnel, formations, coverage and pressure numbers are missing for now because that data is only released after the season."}}